# Every text file checks out with Unix line endings, on every machine, whatever git is set to locally.
#
# **Git for Windows converts line endings on checkout by default** (`core.autocrlf=true`), so without this file each
# machine decided for itself what a tester's copy looked like: two people on one release could hold byte-different
# copies of the same workflow, and only one of them would meet a defect. On 23 September 2026 a run stopped at stage 1
# because a document carried Windows line endings, and the parser's closing fence was matched by exact string
# equality. That half is fixed where files are read, which covers anything a person writes or is sent. This is the
# other half: what we ship arrives identical everywhere.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, and every file it decides is text checks out with `eol=lf`. No tracked
# file carries a carriage return today, so this changes no committed byte; `C186` refuses the first one that tries.
* text=auto eol=lf
